Weston 16.0.0 pixman-renderer: revalidate the shm buffer mapping before painting

Target: the 16.0.0 release tarball pinned by scripts/ci/setup-weston.sh
(sha256 6a81af51..., weston-16.0.0.tar.gz).  The same code is present on
upstream main as of 2026-10-30: pixman_renderer_attach() calls
pixman_image_create_bits() with wl_shm_buffer_get_data() once, and
draw_paint_node() only invalidates the image when the wl_shm_buffer has
disappeared; nothing revalidates the address afterwards.

The bug: wl_shm_pool.resize() (protocol/wayland.xml, interface
wl_shm_pool) tells the server to remap the pool's backing memory so it
can grow.  wayland-server implements shm_pool_resize() ->
shm_pool_finish_resize() -> shm_pool_grow_mapping() with
mremap(..., MREMAP_MAYMOVE) (src/wayland-shm.c), which may move the
mapping to a different address.  wl_shm_buffer_get_data() returns
pool->data + offset and therefore follows the move, but a compositor
that cached the old pointer keeps reading unmapped memory.  Resizing
with live buffers is legal: the protocol only requires the pool to grow
and the client to enlarge the file first ("This request can only be
used to make the pool bigger"; "A buffer will keep a reference to the
pool it was created from so it is valid to destroy the pool immediately
after creating a buffer from it").  Mesa's Vulkan WSI and sctk-adwaita
both grow pools while buffers are attached; sctk-adwaita's decoration
SlotPool growth during a fullscreen transition is what triggers this.

Symptom: repainting the surface whose cached image points at the old
mapping segfaults inside pixman (observed:
fast_composite_scaled_bilinear_sse2_8888_8888_pad_OVER <-
pixman_image_composite32 <- composite_whole <- repaint_region), which
kills the compositor and with it every client connection.

Why wl_shm_buffer_ref_pool is the documented mechanism, and why this
patch does not use it: wayland-server defers the move while
pool->external_refcount > 0, and that count is raised by
wl_shm_buffer_ref_pool() (wayland-server-core.h; see the comment in
shm_pool_resize(): "If the compositor has taken references on this pool
it may be caching pointers into it. In that case we defer the resize").
But the deferral keeps pool->size at the old value, and
shm_pool_create_buffer() rejects offsets beyond pool->size ("invalid
width, height or stride"), so a client that grows a pool and
immediately allocates buffers in the new region -- exactly what
sctk-adwaita and Mesa's WSI do -- gets WL_SHM_ERROR_INVALID_STRIDE
(wayland even logs "Buffer address requested when its parent pool has
an external reference and a deferred resize pending").  Pinning the
pool per cached image would therefore break the clients this is meant
to protect.  The robust fix is to revalidate the address at use, as
below; a deeper alternative is for wayland-server to accept buffer
creation anywhere below new_size while a resize is deferred.

Fix: remember the address the image was created against
(pixman_surface_state.image_data) and rebuild the image from
wl_shm_buffer_get_data() before painting when the pool moved.  This
also covers a deferred move: the new address is picked up on the next
paint.

Regression test: crates/neomacs-gui-tests/tests/wayland_shm_pool_growth.rs
(a raw protocol client; fails 3/3 on unpatched Weston with SIGSEGV
cores, passes 3/3 patched).

Upstream: not yet filed; the project owner submits.
--- a/libweston/pixman-renderer.c	2026-10-30 02:57:23.056418652 -0400
+++ b/libweston/pixman-renderer.c	2026-10-30 02:57:23.058024038 -0400
@@ -55,6 +55,11 @@
 	struct weston_surface *surface;
 
 	pixman_image_t *image;
+	/* The address `image` was created against.  wl_shm_buffer_get_data()
+	 * follows the pool's current mapping, which a client can move with
+	 * wl_shm_pool.resize (wayland-server mremaps with MREMAP_MAYMOVE), so
+	 * the cached pixman image must be rebuilt when it changes. */
+	void *image_data;
 	struct weston_buffer_reference buffer_ref;
 	struct weston_buffer_release_reference buffer_release_ref;
 
@@ -460,10 +465,44 @@
 	 * terminated. This is a particular use-case and should probably be
 	 * refactored to provide some analogue with the GL-renderer (as in, to
 	 * still maintain the buffer and let the compositor dispose of it). */
-	if (ps->buffer_ref.buffer && !ps->buffer_ref.buffer->shm_buffer) {
-		pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
-		ps->image = NULL;
-		return;
+	if (ps->buffer_ref.buffer) {
+		struct wl_shm_buffer *shm_buffer =
+			ps->buffer_ref.buffer->shm_buffer;
+
+		if (!shm_buffer) {
+			pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
+			ps->image = NULL;
+			ps->image_data = NULL;
+			return;
+		}
+
+		/* A client may grow the buffer's shm pool at any time.
+		 * wayland-server then mremaps the pool with MREMAP_MAYMOVE,
+		 * which can move the mapping; wl_shm_buffer_get_data()
+		 * follows the new mapping but the pixman image created at
+		 * attach still points at the old, unmapped address.  Rebuild
+		 * the image when the address it was created against moved. */
+		if (wl_shm_buffer_get_data(shm_buffer) != ps->image_data) {
+			struct weston_buffer *buffer = ps->buffer_ref.buffer;
+			const struct pixel_format_info *pixel_info =
+				pixel_format_get_info_shm(
+					wl_shm_buffer_get_format(shm_buffer));
+			void *data = wl_shm_buffer_get_data(shm_buffer);
+
+			pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
+			ps->image = NULL;
+			if (pixel_info &&
+			    pixman_format_supported_source(pixel_info->pixman_format))
+				ps->image = pixman_image_create_bits(
+					pixel_info->pixman_format,
+					buffer->width, buffer->height,
+					data, buffer->stride);
+			if (!ps->image) {
+				ps->image_data = NULL;
+				return;
+			}
+			ps->image_data = data;
+		}
 	}
 
 	pixman_region32_init(&repaint);
@@ -661,6 +700,7 @@
 		pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
 		ps->image = NULL;
 	}
+	ps->image_data = NULL;
 
 	ps->buffer_destroy_listener.notify = NULL;
 }
@@ -681,6 +721,7 @@
 		pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
 		ps->image = NULL;
 	}
+	ps->image_data = NULL;
 
 	ps->image = pixman_image_create_solid_fill(&color);
 }
@@ -709,6 +750,7 @@
 		pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
 		ps->image = NULL;
 	}
+	ps->image_data = NULL;
 
 	if (!buffer)
 		return;
@@ -756,6 +798,8 @@
 		buffer->width, buffer->height,
 		wl_shm_buffer_get_data(shm_buffer),
 		buffer->stride);
+	ps->image_data = ps->image ?
+		wl_shm_buffer_get_data(shm_buffer) : NULL;
 
 	ps->buffer_destroy_listener.notify =
 		buffer_state_handle_buffer_destroy;
@@ -779,6 +823,7 @@
 		pixman_image_unref(ps->image);
 		ps->image = NULL;
 	}
+	ps->image_data = NULL;
 	weston_buffer_reference(&ps->buffer_ref, NULL,
 				BUFFER_WILL_NOT_BE_ACCESSED);
 	weston_buffer_release_reference(&ps->buffer_release_ref, NULL);
